Jeff Cortez writes:
>How do you get more than one holy water when you have one holy water and a 
>number of waters?

Quick Answer: Dip (#dip) *all* of the normal water potions into the holy water.

Detail: The way that dipping works is as follows: first you specify the
letter of the item(s) in your inventory to be dipped.  Then, you specify the
letter of the potion to dip into.  Only *one* of the holy water potions will
be used up, but *all* of the items specified first will be blessed.  This
allows you to bless all of a bunch of arrows or darts at once, but also works
for any other item (like potions) that gets displayed in groups.

A word on how nethack groups things: things that (if identified) would not be
identical will never be lumped together.  So, if you pick up 3 scrolls of
identify, one cursed, one uncursed, and one blessed, they will all be given
different inventory slots.  Further, any identical items that have been
partially or completely identified will be given different slots.  So if you
have 3 blessed scrolls of enchant weapon, drop one on an altar, and identify
another, you will see them in three different slots.  Finally, items are not
always displayed correctly conglomerated (for instance, when you clear
potions using a unicorn horn), so sometimes you need to dump all the items
you suspect are the same on the floor together to see if they actually do
conglomerate.

Here are several tactics to draw from the above:
1) Always gather as many identical items as you can before blessing them.
2) Keep items you are collecting in the same state of identification.  For
	example, say you are collecting identify scrolls to bless them.  Even
	if you got a free identify (say from a throne) you should *not* use
	it on the scrolls, because then all new identify scrolls you find
	will not be lumped with them!
3) When you are robbing shops using the trained pet method, make an initial
	pass throught the shop on your own to conglomerate items into groups.
	Pets *can* carry groups of things, so this speeds up the process.
4) You can sometimes get information about common things by how they 
	conglomerate.  For instance, when you pick up two clear potions in
	a shop, and they both cost ~126 gp, but they don't group, then you
	know that one is holy water, and the other cursed.

